g to step up to the plate and  whether or not we can work with them  will very much depend upon how they  treat all afghans what does that space  look like how long do we need to wait to  see whether they've changed how many  women need to be raped how many uh girls  need to be married off at the age of 12  how many women need to be told that they  can't be educated past the university  level um none of that is appropriate of  course it's all that would be beyond the  pale but i think again i would ask you  to show the evidence where that's  happening in kabul at the moment  yeah what my question is is how long do  we need to wait how many women need to  be raped or murdered or beaten because  they go out i come back  to you  show us the evidence of that actually  happening on the ground at the moment  well as the taliban returns to  afghanistan there are fears that two  decades of progress for women could be  lost this includes access to education  and employment well nilufa rahmani  became the first female fixed-wing pilot  in the afghan air force she was granted  asylum to the u.s three years ago after  receiving threats from the taliban and  her parents and sister are still in  kabul uh well nila joins me now uh i  suppose the first question you must be  how how are your family in kabul what  contact have you been able to have with  them  well it has been definitely difficult  for them the situation is every day  getting more dangerous and everybody's  so terrified everybody's just finding a  way to get away and unfortunately  i have a difficult time reaching out my  parents i don't know if it is due to  internet or electric or  i have no idea but i am definitely  um really worried about everybody down  there it looks like people are so  terrified they're so scared nilofer tell  us a little bit about  about your journey to where you are now  i mean becoming a pilot in the afghan  air force that uh you know despite the  fact that the taliban were not in power  th