dered futile for  former royal engineer jack cummings the  war was utterly life-changing he lost  both his legs and was in a coma for a  month  well he wrote this tweet on saturday as  the taliban advanced  was it worth it probably not did i lose  my legs for nothing looks like it did my  mates die in vain  yep  many emotions going through my head  anger betrayal sadness  to name a few  well jack joins me now  can you put it into words how you're  feeling watch afghanistan go back 20 30  40 years  no honestly i'm totally loss of words  totally lost the words what's happened  the past week the past couple of days  you know just  yeah the emotions are all even more so  you went over when i wrote the tweet on  saturday it was it was that as a  certainty as my anniversary it was my  anniversary and blown up as us scotty's  called your bangiversary so that day's  always raw for me anyway raw for me and  my family normally i'm quite quiet i uh  keep myself to myself you know i mean my  wife will have a few drinks and  reminisce but i just felt on saturday i  had to write something that i had to put  my two pence and just seeing events  unfolds in afghanistan just  heartbreaking and devastating to see  just the country i fought in and fought  for  has just just gone up in smoke like that  you know i'm saying my best wishes the  600 powers that have gone out and i you  know i hope they all come home safe and  sound and they bring home  as many as possible including as many  interpreters as they can get hold of you  know if anybody deserves the right to be  free and and live in peace and come to  the uk as the interpreters that helped  western forces and i i really hope they  can get as many as possible i feel sorry  for the country of afghanistan um  you know my job out there was to find  bombs to find night improvised explosive  devices i love my job  i'm so proud to have served in the  british military in the british army  do you think a presence should have  remained because the excuse that  president biden used was  we don't want a forever war  i i get that to a degree i truly do and  i'm not naive i knew we couldn't stay  here forever afghanistan and afghans got  the right to look after themselves and  govern themselves and to look after  themselves but just to see  the quick withdrawal and then it to go  up and smoke in a week or whatever it  was it just just doesn't feel right and  it that can't be right i just wonder  what you would say to  boris johnson if he's watching now  to to boris johnson um me personally was  it worth it  you know he said you know british  soldiers didn't die in vain but i don't  think he'll ever say that face to face  of a grieving mum or a grieving wife or  grieving father  um  you know i lost my legs and i will never  walk again and i'm bound to a wheelchair  for the rest of my life don't get me  wrong i knew the rest i i signed up on  the deadline and i knew the risk going  out i was proud to serve my country i  knew that but i wanted something out of  afghanistan i truly felt i was making a  difference in afghanistan every bomb  that i was finding was saying their life  these bombs don't discriminate these  ideas don't discriminate it doesn't  matter if you're a british serviceman  american or you're you're a local afghan  if you stand then it's going bang and  you're you're losing a leg you're losing  alarm you're losing your life everyone i  found was hoping to make a difference  how do the families of those who didn't  come home feel about the taliban's  takeover my next guest christine  harrison is the mother of corporal  darren bonner who was killed in helmand  in may of 2007 she feels that down and  that everything they achieved was for  nothing hello to you thank you for  joining us christine i've just been  speaking to the head of the army and he  was saying that your son's life and  those of his colleagues were not in vain  what would you say in response to him  um what i'd say i mean i would have said  that as um  going back a few months ago but i mean  i've just come to terms really with the  fact that darren's death has freed  people and that and that  it's taken me 14 years  to get to that point  um and now i just feel it was for  nothing i i don't i don't agree with  what the government is saying  um  i'm very very proud of my son don't get  me wrong i'm i love you know love him i  miss him  and  there's other families out there like me  who feel like  feel like this and heartbroken i feel  like i've been taken back 14 years  um and i'm reliving it all over again  and um  the government can't take that away  and  i'm sorry  um  other families  are in the same boat as i am and  and it was in vain  i haven't got my son  and he died i feel right now  he died for nothing  um yes i would have said he  he done everything he could i'm very  very proud of him and he was very very  proud of being part of the british army  but um  i feel so let down so let down and i  feel he's been let down as so many other  soldiers have  [Music]
 

